{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: the owned-row frontier context bound for the Room A archive pass at exact head 20a0edfff06ee5f0f2732f48591b22748395d32a, tree 5d7cf47632139fff86473887282dcdec58560961, on builder/archive-owned-row-context-20261025 (review request 88887458, my promise 64dc7632). This review binds child request 07f2de13 (builder promise 2e6f5f5f) to this one candidate as the local code part; parent request 1aedd35b stays open for planner deployment and hosted Room A observation, which this head does not claim. The head may land on Main 6c0a9a4d: it sits directly on Main (four commits, fast-forward, git merge-tree returns the head's own tree), and gate 52302 at 02a22b5a stands under the documents-only rule because the head differs from the gated commit only in the implementation note (+9/-3 lines, no other path).\n\nRead order: the primary note first and in full, then the review request, the child request and the builder's promise, the parent's planner report 8a89a36d with the hosted figures; then the production diff (archive-frontier-cost.ts, archive-adaptive.ts, archive-driver.ts), the frontier-store test, the seal map, and the surrounding code at the head (the frontier store's measurement and refusal, the forecast's use of the bound, the row byte cap); then the evidence index, the gate's raw record and the audits it names.\n\nHead checks: the worktree /private/tmp/artroom-builder-archive-room-a-plan-refusal-20261025 is clean at the head on its branch; merge-base with Main is Main; 6 files, +126/-20; git diff --check is clean; check-commit-messages reports 4 checked, 0 flagged; the 6 artifact events at this head name exactly the 6 changed paths; the note blob at the head is 9,365 bytes with the SHA256 the index records (16821ad3…).\n\nProduction correctness, from the code at the head:\n- The bound. archiveFrontierRowContextBound keeps its generic ceiling, min(32 KiB, 2048 + 6 per unit of the at, repository and confirmed strings), and with an owned size returns min(generic, 1024 + owned bytes + 32). The owned size must be a natural number, non-zero and at most ARCHIVE_ROW_BYTES, or the pass stops as unavailable. Only a consumed same-owner planning snapshot supplies it: the adaptive planner passes the consumed byte count or undefined, so standalone callers, the fallback serialization path and pending rebuilds (the driver's rebuild branch never sets a selected bound) keep the generic ceiling.\n- Ceiling, not authority. The frontier store still measures the three sub-objects with SQL octet_length under the fixed 1024, 25600 and 1024 caps, refuses \"limit\" when 1024 plus the measured sum exceeds the bound, and keeps the identity predicates and compare-and-set in the projection query; #prepare refuses any bound outside 1024 to 32 KiB, and the owned bound is at least 1,057. A changed or enlarged row is refused, not admitted.\n- Soundness. The stored row is the canonical text and json_extract returns each sub-object's text without added whitespace, so the measured at, repository and confirmed bytes together stay below the whole row's canonical size; the 32 bytes cover the anchorsPublished spelling admitted at promotion. The test exercises quote, backslash, escaped controls, BMP and astral characters in the repository and asserts 1024 plus the measured sum is at most the bound before and after promotion.\n- Charged once. The adaptive planner spends metadata 128 and inputWork 256 once before lookahead; finishCost takes the bound as a parameter and adds no fee. The forecast depends on the bound at 2 by 5 metadata and 2 by 13 inputWork per byte, which the test asserts exactly along with unchanged entries, attempts, nodes and calls; the store's actual charge is 4 plus one native per byte of metadata and 12 plus one of inputWork, so forecast and charge move together.\n- Coupled. The successful adaptive result carries rowContextBytes and the driver passes it to prepareFromSource, so actual preparation uses the forecast's bound; the fallback uses the generic bound from the row, as before.\n- Unchanged. ARCHIVE_BOUNDS (entries 4096, attempts 512, nodes 64, calls 16384, metadata 4 MiB, inputWork 64 MiB), the tariffs, the snapshot custody in archive-row.ts (its seal is unchanged), the SQL fences and the Mark and inventory checks are not in the diff.\n- Recorded bytes. As in my review of 74e34d2e: the native Mark manifest records cost, so receipts can change and a pass may pick a different cutoff; the public v2 manifest carries no cost; pending Marks rebuild through the generic path. The note says exactly this.\n- Hosted arithmetic, conditional. For the reconstructed 621-byte hosted row the bound is 1,677 against a generic 4,016 (328 units), a net saving of 23,262 metadata and 60,558 inputWork after the fee; against the measured shortfall of 10,423 that leaves 12,839 bytes. This depends on the hosted row size and is not a fit claim; the planner deploys and reads the next tail.\n\nQualified evidence, verified: the bound evidence index (5,116 bytes, SHA256 8f8c9390…) and all eight entries, the five gate raw records and every leaf listed by the eight manifests exist with the stated sizes and digests. The raw gate record has expectedHead 02a22b5a, expectedTree 6d684553, unchangedCheckpoint true, gatePassed true, exit 0, errors [], before.json identical to after.json; stdout shows eight stages at exit 0, \"Tests 3580 passed | 3 skipped (3583)\" over 499 passed and 1 skipped files, Node 42 and Counting 55; the copied typecheck log holds 40 tsc contexts and no TypeScript error; the 17 stage copies verify against their originals. The independent gate audit records the same head and tree, 40 affected cases all passed, unique final IDs and zero unhandled errors. The 50706 receipt records head 9ee19630, two compile contexts and 40 of 40 cases over four files (row 9, frontier-store 4, driver 19, installed 8). Control 30424 replaced only the final narrowing at mutant 90ca434d and failed as intended at archive-frontier-store.test.ts:203:31 (\"expected 2948 to be less than 2948\") with the other three cases passing, and the positive source was restored at a8a82a0b, tree 6c280ade, whose target digest equals the proposal's final frontier-cost source; I read these records and did not rerun them. The driver seal 82da30f6… reproduces from the maintained producer at the head, the archive-row seal is unchanged, archive-adaptive.ts and archive-frontier-cost.ts are not in the seal map, and scripts/security-entry-points.test.mjs passes 4 of 4 at the head in my own run (1.24 s wall). The Counting figure is Node coverage; no browser or hosted check is claimed and none ran.\n\nNonblocking notes:\n- The 12,839-byte margin at the reconstructed row is thin against the planner's warning that the next Grow entry shape is likely larger; the next tail decides whether a second cut is needed under the parent.\n- The 128/256 fee is also paid on the fallback path where no narrowing happens; it is small and the note describes it as a fixed charge. Fine as written.\n\nArchitecture: the tighter bound comes from a fact the pass has already paid for and checked, the owned canonical row, and is computed in the cost module, consumed by the adaptive planner and carried by the driver to the store, so forecast and actual preparation share one number. The generic ceiling stays as the fallback for every caller without a consumed snapshot. No new module, no new state.\n\nSecurity: no authority changes. A size hint cannot admit a changed row: the SQL measurements, identity predicates and compare-and-set in the frontier store still run and refuse above the bound; the fixed caps, prices, custody token and recorded bytes are unchanged; the diagnostic path is untouched. The seal map changes only for the driver and the value reproduces.\n\nSimplification: nothing to remove; threading one parameter through finishCost and one local through the driver is the minimal shape.\n\nFindings: none blocking.","body":{